Issue: The delivery ladder gives up the complete demonstration set on the *first* refusal, and a refusal is a verdict the provider has been observed to change its mind about: in the parent run's own traces the same prompt, byte for byte, was refused for one sample of LawBench example 19 and answered for its sibling, yet the harness treated the single refusal as decisive and was sticky about it, so 6 of the tip run's 100 LawBench samples (examples 4, 6, 7) ran their whole panel at 5, 7 or 88 demonstrated examples instead of the 200 the memory holds — and a refusal at the last rung is still an ordinary failure, so `if not results: raise` loses the sample outright, which is exactly the hole the tip node's notes flagged as untested ("the mechanism is untested against a refusal that survives all three rungs, in which case the sample is lost exactly as the parent lost it").

Modification: `REFUSALS_PER_RUNG = 2` in `solver/agent.py`, and `_step_down` becomes `_refused_request`: a refusal is charged to the rung it arrived at, and the attempt is repeated — the same question, at the same rung, with the same demonstration set — while that rung has been refused fewer than `REFUSALS_PER_RUNG` times; only a size the provider has refused that many times is given up, one rung at a time, and a refusal at the smallest size (or one the panel still has to answer with) keeps its previous meaning. A refused request still counts as neither a draw nor a panel failure, grounding, the vote, `PANEL_SIZE`, `PANEL_BUDGET`, `PANEL_FAILURES` and memory preparation are untouched, and a run whose requests are all read behaves exactly as before.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3333, "Symptom2Disease": 0.89, "LawBench": 0.57, "average": 0.5978, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The expected effect was not achieved, and the run measures why. The mechanism executed exactly as designed — the refused samples each spent one extra request at the rung that refused them (LawBench 0004 and 0006: 7 attempts / 3 draws where the parent had 5 / 3, i.e. 4 refusals instead of 2; 0007: 10 / 8 and 5 / 3 against the parent's 9 / 8 and 9 / 8) — and every one of the 10 retries was refused as well, so the delivery stepped down at exactly the same places: the rung each of the 260 samples was asked at is identical to the parent's, 94 LawBench samples at the complete set and the same 6 samples of examples 4, 6 and 7 at the same reduced sizes. The refusal in this run is therefore a repeated verdict on a (question, size) pair and not a property of a single request, which the tip run's example 19 did not distinguish. All 260 samples were answered (completion 1.0, rankable: true, status: ok) and the average moved 0.5933 -> 0.5978, but that is churn, not the mechanism: the flips are one-sample votes on questions the lineage has always flipped (USPTO 20/60 against 18/60 with 0013 split +1/-1, Symptom2Disease 89/100 against 90/100 on 0047 sample-1, LawBench 57/100 against 58/100 with 0028 sample-1 and 0007 sample-0 out and 0045 sample-0 in), and no sample's prompt differs from the parent's in any way the mechanism is responsible for. The cost of the retries is bounded and visible: 10 extra refused requests (no tokens, ~10s each) and LawBench's refusals 10 -> 20, with the run's total attempts still lower than the parent's (1060 against 1139) because this run met no retryable provider failures at all (0 retried attempts against 29)."}

